1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,613
Hi, I'm Aravind, and this is a synthetic
voice reading the script.

2
00:00:04,613 --> 00:00:07,739
This film is about my final project,
Klaxon.

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:11,213
Klaxon is an AI on-call engineer for a
payments system.

4
00:00:11,213 --> 00:00:14,031
When something breaks, it works out what
went wrong,

5
00:00:14,031 --> 00:00:17,444
shows its evidence, and asks a person
before changing anything.

6
00:00:17,444 --> 00:00:21,432
Then it checks what a dashboard can miss:
whether the money is right.

7
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:23,945
That last check is why it matters.

8
00:00:23,945 --> 00:00:25,897
In one run on my own test system,

9
00:00:25,897 --> 00:00:29,684
a bad deploy made the ledger count some
payments more than once.

10
00:00:29,684 --> 00:00:31,754
The other alert rules stayed quiet.

11
00:00:31,754 --> 00:00:36,796
Only the money check failed: our books
were $461 ahead of the payment provider's.

12
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:40,653
An engineer, or an AI agent, could close
an incident like that

13
00:00:40,653 --> 00:00:44,222
because everything looks healthy, while
the books are still wrong.

14
00:00:44,222 --> 00:00:47,555
With Klaxon, a fix isn't done until the
money is checked.

15
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:53,095
The industry calls this an AI SRE, an AI
site reliability engineer.

16
00:00:53,095 --> 00:00:58,273
Resolve AI raised $125 million at a $1
billion valuation in February,

17
00:00:58,273 --> 00:01:01,876
Traversal $120 million in November, and
Datadog,

18
00:01:01,876 --> 00:01:04,978
AWS and Microsoft now offer their own.

19
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:06,772
What did they get?

20
00:01:06,772 --> 00:01:12,643
Microsoft says its agent has mitigated
over 35,000 incidents in its own services.

21
00:01:12,643 --> 00:01:17,283
But on ITBench, a research benchmark,
agents on state-of-the-art

22
00:01:17,283 --> 00:01:21,013
models resolved only 11.4% of its SRE
scenarios.

23
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:25,610
The open-source agent I looked at,
HolmesGPT, can check a deploy by

24
00:01:25,610 --> 00:01:29,659
asking a model to compare error rates,
latency, restarts and logs.

25
00:01:29,659 --> 00:01:34,081
In its docs, I didn't find a built-in
check that the money is right.

26
00:01:34,567 --> 00:01:35,612
So why build mine?

27
00:01:35,612 --> 00:01:39,561
Klaxon is a small copy of this idea, with
the check I couldn't find:

28
00:01:39,561 --> 00:01:42,523
after a fix, code, not the model, checks
the money.

29
00:01:42,523 --> 00:01:45,910
And Klaxon changes nothing until a person
approves it.

30
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:48,230
It all runs on synthetic data.

31
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:51,706
A small payments system runs in Docker on
my Mac: an API,

32
00:01:51,706 --> 00:01:56,402
a fake provider that sends signed
webhooks, Kafka, and a double-entry ledger.

33
00:01:56,402 --> 00:01:58,476
Simulated customers make payments,

34
00:01:58,476 --> 00:02:02,202
and six faults with known causes are
injected on purpose.

35
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:06,529
When an alert fires, Klaxon asks Claude
for one step at a time:

36
00:02:06,529 --> 00:02:11,392
call one of eight read-only tools, served
by an MCP server written from scratch,

37
00:02:11,392 --> 00:02:15,046
or give a diagnosis that cites the tool
calls behind it.

38
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:17,592
Here is a real run from week seven.

39
00:02:17,592 --> 00:02:21,240
The money alert fired, and in 25 seconds
and three tool calls,

40
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:24,182
Klaxon found the cause: a ledger deploy
that books

41
00:02:24,182 --> 00:02:26,888
every message without checking for
duplicates.

42
00:02:26,888 --> 00:02:29,138
It asked to roll that deploy back.

43
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:33,708
I approved it by typing its id in my own
terminal, 70 seconds later.

44
00:02:33,708 --> 00:02:36,883
Code then checked the result: healthy,
cause removed,

45
00:02:36,883 --> 00:02:40,848
but the money still wrong, because a
rollback repairs nothing.

46
00:02:41,333 --> 00:02:43,868
Weeks one to seven are built and public.

47
00:02:43,868 --> 00:02:48,620
Planned by December 4th: a multi-agent
version, the webhook receiver in Go,

48
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:52,164
a 72-run comparison of the loops, and a
safety pass.

49
00:02:52,667 --> 00:02:53,877
Do I have what I need?

50
00:02:53,877 --> 00:02:57,453
There is no API key and no token credits:
every call goes through

51
00:02:57,453 --> 00:03:00,974
my university's Claude seat, using Claude
Code in headless mode.

52
00:03:00,974 --> 00:03:05,375
It isn't billed per call, but it has
usage limits, so big batches run overnight.

53
00:03:05,375 --> 00:03:08,676
Dollar figures are estimates at API
prices, not money spent.

54
00:03:08,676 --> 00:03:11,072
Compute is my Mac and GitHub's free CI.

55
00:03:11,567 --> 00:03:14,928
I made the decisions, reviewed and
accepted every week,

56
00:03:14,928 --> 00:03:16,883
and typed every approval myself.

57
00:03:16,883 --> 00:03:19,945
Claude Code wrote the code under my
direction.

58
00:03:20,433 --> 00:03:21,819
How will I know it works?

59
00:03:21,819 --> 00:03:25,587
Eight test cases, each with its right
answer written before any run:

60
00:03:25,587 --> 00:03:30,326
the six faults, a false alarm, and a
fault where the tools can't see the deploys.

61
00:03:30,833 --> 00:03:31,901
I check three things.

62
00:03:31,901 --> 00:03:33,273
Did it name the real cause?

63
00:03:33,273 --> 00:03:37,035
A keyword rule written in advance and a
Claude judge that never sees which

64
00:03:37,035 --> 00:03:40,492
model wrote it both grade that, checked
against my own blind labels.

65
00:03:40,492 --> 00:03:42,627
Did it name an action only where one fits?

66
00:03:42,627 --> 00:03:47,147
And when I approve an action, does code
find the system healthy and the money right?

67
00:03:47,633 --> 00:03:51,111
The first results, from 48 runs on two
Claude models:

68
00:03:51,111 --> 00:03:55,442
Opus named the cause in all 18 of its
fault runs, by both graders.

69
00:03:55,442 --> 00:03:58,251
No run named an action where none fits.

70
00:03:58,767 --> 00:04:02,531
What didn't work yet: on the false alarm,
no run named an action,

71
00:04:02,531 --> 00:04:07,338
but each grader found only one run in six
that said plainly that nothing was wrong.

72
00:04:07,338 --> 00:04:10,060
And every fault was known while I built
Klaxon,

73
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:13,438
so this is not yet a test on faults it
has never seen.

74
00:04:13,933 --> 00:04:17,275
So it works if the 72 comparison runs
keep naming causes

75
00:04:17,275 --> 00:04:20,676
with no wrong actions, the false alarm
gets called false,

76
00:04:20,676 --> 00:04:24,078
and code keeps catching fixes that leave
the money wrong.

77
00:04:24,078 --> 00:04:28,016
It fails on a confident wrong cause, or
an action where none fits.

78
00:04:28,016 --> 00:04:30,354
Klaxon, by Aravind Sundaravadivelu.
